menu "Metroseq (Tulip CC CV sequencer)"

    config METRO_DAC_I2C_HZ
        int "I2C clock for the Mabee DAC writes (Hz)"
        default 400000
        range 100000 400000
        help
            SCL speed used for every GP8413 transaction. 400 kHz makes a
            gate/pitch write ~4x shorter than 100 kHz (about 0.1 ms vs 0.4 ms),
            which is the latency between the scheduled time and the voltage
            actually changing. Tulip's own mabeedac.py uses 400 kHz on this
            bus. Drop to 100000 only if your cable/pull-ups are marginal.

    config METRO_TOUCH_I2C_HZ
        int "I2C clock for the GT911 touch controller (Hz)"
        default 100000
        range 100000 400000
        help
            Tulip's firmware runs the touch controller at 100 kHz on the R11
            board. Touch reads only happen in gaps between CV writes, so a
            slower touch clock never delays a gate.

    config METRO_CV_SET_RANGE_10V
        bool "Explicitly set each GP8413 to its 0-10 V range at init"
        default y
        help
            Writes 0x11 to the GP8413 range register so 1 V/oct pitch is
            correct regardless of the chip's power-on range. Turn off to leave
            the range as the board ships (the Python sketch's default).

    config METRO_DEFER_FLASH_WHILE_PLAYING
        bool "Commit preset/project saves to flash only when stopped"
        default y
        help
            Saving always updates the in-RAM bank immediately. With this on,
            the flash write waits until the transport is stopped, so no flash
            erase/program ever overlaps a running sequence. (With XIP from
            PSRAM enabled the CPU keeps running during flash writes anyway;
            this is a second layer of protection.)

    config METRO_TIMING_PROBE_GPIO
        int "Scope timing probe GPIO (-1 = off)"
        default -1
        help
            Mirrors TRK1's gate onto this pin at the instant the I2C write
            starts, so a scope on probe + DAC VOUT0 shows scheduling jitter
            and I2C latency separately. GPIO11 (the unused MIDI OUT pin) is a
            convenient choice; it will send garbage on MIDI OUT while enabled.

endmenu
